%I A400150 #32 Sep 27 2026 04:37:22
%S A400150 0,1,0,3,4,3,0,5,8,9,8,5,0,7,12,15,16,15,12,7,0,9,16,21,24,25,24,21,
%T A400150 16,9,0,11,20,27,32,35,36,35,32,27,20,11,0,13,24,33,40,45,48,49,48,45,
%U A400150 40,33,24,13,0,15,28,39,48,55,60,63,64,63,60,55,48,39
%N A400150 a(n) = |2*a(n-1) - a(n-2) - 2|, with a(0) = 0 and a(1) = 1.
%C A400150 Progressively larger integer parabolas that start from 0 and return to 0 after every arch. General formula: a(n) = |2*a(n-1) - a(n-2) - c| for n >= 2, with a(0) = 0 and a(1) = 1, where c = 1 or 2. For this sequence, c = 2.
%C A400150 Physics interpretation: a(n) gives the height of a point particle at time step n. The particle is subjected to a''(n) = -c, and a'(n+1) = |a'(n)| + c after each collision with the boundary at a(n) = 0. For initial values a(0) = 0 and a(1) = 1, and integer c > 0, c = 1 or 2. Proof: suppose the first collision with the boundary happens at the j-th step, a(j) = 0, j > 0. Then a(j) = Sum_{n=1..j} [1 - (n-1)*c] = j - c*j*(j-1)/2. j = 1+2/c. Since j is an integer, c = 1 or 2.
%C A400150 For c = 1, the resulting sequence is |A323186(n)|, and the maxima of the parabolic arches are the triangular numbers (cf. A000217). For c = 2 (this sequence), the maxima of the arches are the square numbers (cf. A000290). Let k >= 0 index each arch, the zeros occur at n = k*(k+1), the oblong numbers (cf. A002378).
%H A400150 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A400150/b400150.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..9999</a>
%F A400150 a(n) = (n-k*(k+1)) * (2*k+2 - n + k*(k+1)), where k = floor((sqrt(4*n+1)-1)/2).
%e A400150 Using the iteration rule,
%e A400150 a(2) = |2*a(1) - a(0) - 2| = |2*1 - 0 - 2| = 0.
%e A400150 a(3) = |2*a(2) - a(1) - 2| = |2*0 - 1 - 2| = 3.
%e A400150 a(4) = |2*a(3) - a(2) - 2| = |2*3 - 0 - 2| = 4.
%e A400150 Using the closed-form formula for n = 4, so k = 1, and a(4) = (4-1*2) * (2*1+2-4+1*2) = 2*2 = 4.
%t A400150 A400150[n_] := (n - #*(# + 1))*(#*(# + 3) - n + 2) & [Quotient[Sqrt[4*n + 1] - 1, 2]];
%t A400150 Array[A400150, 100, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 27 2026 *)
%t A400150 (* Alternative: *)
%t A400150 Module[{a, n}, RecurrenceTable[{a[n] == Abs[2*a[n-1] - a[n-2] - 2], a[0] == 0, a[1] == 1}, a, {n, 0, 99}]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 27 2026 *)
%o A400150 (Python)
%o A400150 a = [0, 1]
%o A400150 for n in range(2, 100):
%o A400150     a.append(abs(2*a[-1] - a[-2] - 2))
%o A400150 print(a)
%Y A400150 Cf. A323186, A002378, A000290.
%Y A400150 Cf. A003991 (multiplication table read by antidiagonals; the nonzero terms of each arch are successive odd-numbered rows), A094053 (triangle read by rows; the current sequence is 0 followed by the even-numbered rows).
%K A400150 nonn,easy,look,new
%O A400150 0,4
%A A400150 _Eddie Lin Rui_, Sep 20 2026
